  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally calm conditions this weekend ahead of the \nnext cold front. \n\nEarly morning surface analysis shows that the slow-moving cold front \nhas finally made it south of our local area. It has left light \nnortheast winds and an abundance of scattered low to mid clouds in \nits wake. Hi-res models continue to show showers developing through \ndaybreak, especially across eastern VA, but radar trends have been \nmuch quieter than expected so far overnight. There are a few\nradar returns trying to develop as of this writing in Louisa and\nFluvanna Counties, as well as across the Middle Peninsula. Will\nkeep the chance of rain in the forecast through 8-9am due to \nmodel persistence and expanding low cloud coverage. A broad \nsurface high pressure will move across southeastern Canada into \nNew England today resulting in a rather benign mid-Septmber \nday. Skies will remain partly to mostly cloudy as high \ntemperatures top out in the upper 70s to around 80 degrees \nacross western locations and the Eastern Shore, with lower 80s \nalong the I-95 corridor and east. Dewpoints should be able to \nfall a few degrees into the mid to upper 60s, so it shouldn't \nfeel quite as sticky outside as it did yesterday. Can't rule out\na light shower across the Eastern Shore this afternoon. Areas \ncloser to the coast will see wind gusts of 12-15mph this \nafternoon. \n\nThe area of high pressure moves offshore by Sunday morning as the \nnext cold front pushes into the Ohio River Valley. Winds will become \nsouth/southwesterly allowing temperatures to warm back up into mid \nto upper 80s (lower 80s for the Eastern Shore). This will be our \nlast day of widespread 80s for at least this current forecast \nperiod. Expect to see more breaks of sunshine, especially across \nsouthern counties. The front will nudge closer to our northern/western\nCWA border by late Sunday potentially bringing some showers \nalong with it. Sunday night low temperatures will be a smidge \nmilder in the mid to upper 60s as cloud cover and scattered rain\nchances return. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Persistent onshore flow and a potentially \nstrengthening coastal low will create a wetter, cooler, and breezier \nforecast next week.  \n\nBig changes are in store beginning Monday as a much more unsettled \npattern sets its eyes on the eastern US for much of the week. The \naforementioned cold front will slowly move through our area late \nMonday into Tuesday as a large ridge of high pressure becomes \nestablished across the Great Lakes into the Hudson Bay Region \nof Canada. The slow progression of the front will allow for \nrain/storm chances to persist through the day on Monday. \nHowever, it should be noted that rainfall totals have trended \ndownwards for Monday. WPC has also removed the Slight Risk for \nExcessive Rainfall, but keeping a Marginal Risk in place. \nNortheast winds will become breezy later in the day, especially \nfor coastal locations. Northern counties behind the front will \nsee high temperatures in the mid to upper 70s. To the south, one\nlast day of mid 80s are expected - our far southern NC counties\ncould even see some upper 80s. \n\nUpper ridge shifts a bit eastward on Tuesday as onshore flow \nstrengthens for us here locally. Northeast winds look to increase\nfor eastern areas during the day on Tuesday, likely lasting \nthrough at least Thursday as a low pressure system develops and \nstrengthens over the Atlantic off of the Mid-Atlantic and\nsouthern New England coast. There remain model differences in \nexact placement and strength of this low, but regardless, trends\ncontinue to highlight potentially impactful weather for our \narea - especially along the coast - for multiple days. Rain \nchances will also continue each day through the week, possibly \nleading to periods of localized flooding. WPC has a Slight Risk \nof Excessive Rainfall for most of the area on Tuesday. High \ntemperatures will be below normal for the remainder of the week,\npotentially in the upper 60s to low/mid 70s. Please stay tuned \nto the forecast for next week as this system develops and \npotentially brings several coastal hazards.",
